PAGES.push({id:'how-it-works', group:'Start here', icon:'⚙️', title:'How the model works',
 keywords:'lightgbm platt features method pipeline erddap lag sunday target rolling origin',
 lede:'What the forecast predicts, what it looks at, how it is trained, and how it is kept honest. Method only — no code.',
 html:`
<div class="flow">
 <div class="step"><b>1 · Data</b><span>MI publishes weekly phytoplankton counts on its open ERDDAP server</span></div>
 <div class="step"><b>2 · Sunday cut-off</b><span>Keep only counts actually published before the issue Sunday</span></div>
 <div class="step"><b>3 · 14 features</b><span>Season, location, the station’s own recent counts, neighbours within 30 km</span></div>
 <div class="step"><b>4 · LightGBM</b><span>Gradient-boosted trees give a risk score</span></div>
 <div class="step"><b>5 · Platt scaling</b><span>Recalibrated on the most recent year to give honest probabilities</span></div>
 <div class="step"><b>6 · Issue & log</b><span>P(≥100 cells/L in week +1 or +2), locked before outcomes are known</span></div>
</div>

<h2>What it predicts</h2>
<ul>
 <li><strong>Target:</strong> total <em>Dinophysis</em> spp. (all <em>Dinophysis</em> taxa summed per sample, maximum over the week’s samples) reaching <strong>≥100 cells/L</strong> at an MI station in ISO week <strong>+1 or +2</strong> after the Sunday issue. MI counts come in steps of 40 cells/L, so in practice “≥100” means ≥120 as counted.</li>
 <li><strong>Rows:</strong> one per station sampled in the issue week (week 0). A row is scored only if the station is sampled again in week +1 or +2; unverifiable weeks are excluded, not counted as “no bloom”.</li>
 <li><strong>Why summed species:</strong> it matches how the 100 cells/L trigger is defined, has the most positives, and scores at least as well as a single-species label.</li>
 <li><strong>Add-on:</strong> a separate <em>D. acuta</em> flag (the late-summer, long-closure species) was built for the prototype app.</li>
</ul>

<h2>The weekly cycle and the data lag</h2>
<p>The forecast is issued every <strong>Sunday</strong>. The question that matters operationally is which counts are actually public by then. MI’s phytoplankton dataset carries a publication timestamp for every sample, so we measured it directly:</p>
<div class="cards">
 <div class="stat"><div class="v">3.6 <small>days</small></div><div class="l">Median time from sampling to publication, 2019–26 (90th percentile 6.6–9.6 days)</div></div>
 <div class="stat"><div class="v">78%</div><div class="l">Of a week’s Monday–Saturday samples are public before that Sunday</div></div>
 <div class="stat"><div class="v sm">90 · 75 · 40 · 2%</div><div class="l">Share published by Sunday for Monday · Tuesday · Wednesday · Thursday samples</div></div>
</div>
<p>Publication dates are genuine from mid-May 2018. Earlier samples all carry a single bulk “published” stamp from when the database went live, so they are not used for timing.</p>

<h2>The 14 features</h2>
<p>All features were fixed before any test score was seen.</p>
<div class="tbl"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>Features</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><strong>Season & location (4)</strong></td><td>Week of year as sine and cosine; latitude; longitude</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Station persistence (7)</strong></td><td>log(1 + last count); weeks since that count; maximum log count in the 4 weeks before the cut-off; weeks since the last ≥100 count (capped at 260); number of ≥100 weeks in the prior 52 weeks; same season last year (±3 weeks); last change</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Neighbours (3)</strong></td><td>Other stations within 30 km in the week before the cut-off: maximum log count; fraction ≥100; number sampled</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Missing values are left missing (the tree model handles them natively) rather than filled with zeros.</p>

<h2>The model</h2>
<ul>
 <li><strong>LightGBM</strong> gradient-boosted trees with locked parameters (200 trees, learning rate 0.05, 31 leaves, class-weighted).</li>
 <li><strong>Platt scaling:</strong> the raw scores are recalibrated on the most recent year so that “20%” means roughly one in five. Isotonic and season-adjusted alternatives were tested and did worse (test #53).</li>
 <li>Calibration on 2022–26: mean forecast 4.3% vs 3.9% observed; calibration slope 0.97; Brier score 0.0318 vs 0.0355 for the seasonal norm (Brier skill +0.11).</li>
</ul>

<h2>How it is evaluated</h2>
<ul>
 <li><strong>Rolling origin:</strong> to forecast year Y, the model is fitted on years ≤ Y−2, calibrated on Y−1 and tested on Y, exactly as it would have run at the time. Years 2016–2026 are scored this way.</li>
 <li><strong>Two pools:</strong> the headline pool is <strong>2022–2026</strong> (534 positives in 13,576 station-weeks, prevalence 3.9%); <strong>2016–2021</strong> is a separate check pool.</li>
 <li><strong>Two information settings:</strong> <em>conservative</em> (only counts sampled at least 7 days before the issue: PR-AUC 0.290) and <em>operational</em> (counts actually published by Sunday: PR-AUC 0.312). An earlier “week-0 known” figure of 0.353 was retired after the red team found it included samples taken on the issue Sunday itself.</li>
 <li><strong>Mandatory baselines:</strong> week-of-year climatology (0.124), “same as last observed” (0.144), the station × week seasonal norm (0.231), and later a hierarchical ridge logistic (0.280) and an “active-year” empirical-Bayes norm (0.240).</li>
 <li><strong>Uncertainty:</strong> block bootstraps that resample whole stations, years, region-years, bloom episodes and moving blocks, so that correlated weeks are not treated as independent.</li>
 <li><strong>Pre-registration:</strong> each test’s feature sets, targets and decision rule were committed before scoring. A new input is adopted only if it gains in <em>both</em> pools with P(Δ ≤ 0) &lt; 0.10 in 2022–26. Most candidates failed this rule; that is the point.</li>
</ul>

<h2>From probability to product</h2>
<p>The prototype app turns probabilities into four bands (Low / Watch / Elevated / High). In a pseudo-prospective replay of the 2026 season (5 Apr–13 Sep; 1,157 forecasts, 121 events), the observed bloom rate rose with the band: <strong>Low 0.5%, Watch 12.3%, Elevated 24.5%, High 47.6%</strong>. Each region also carries a skill badge (“demonstrated” only in the South-West). Every issued forecast is written to a <strong>hash-chained log</strong> before outcomes are known, so it cannot be quietly edited later.</p>
<figure><img src="assets/img/persistence_v1.png" alt="Per-year PR-AUC of the persistence model vs baselines 2016-2026 and reliability diagram"><figcaption>The persistence model (the core of v2) against baselines by year, 2016–2026, and its reliability on the 2022–26 pool. The skill of every method moves with the year: busy years are easier. In this figure, “week-0 count known” is the retired optimistic setting.</figcaption></figure>
`});
PAGES.push({id:'results', group:'Findings', icon:'📊', title:'Results & scorecard',
 keywords:'pr-auc headline scorecard per region per year closures brier ridge baseline',
 lede:'The headline numbers, the baselines they are compared against, and how they hold up by region, by year and against shellfish closures.',
 html:`
<h2>Headline scorecard</h2>
<div class="tbl"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Measure (2022–26 unless stated)</th><th class="num">Model</th><th class="num">Baseline</th><th>Gain & uncertainty</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr class="hl"><td><strong>Operational</strong> (counts published by Sunday), national</td><td class="num"><strong>0.312</strong></td><td class="num">0.231 norm</td><td>+0.082; region-year 90% CI +0.016 to +0.110 (95% +0.002 to +0.117)</td></tr>
<tr class="hl"><td><strong>Operational</strong>, South-West</td><td class="num"><strong>0.338</strong></td><td class="num">0.203 norm</td><td>year-block 90% CI +0.010 to +0.172</td></tr>
<tr><td>Operational, year-averaged</td><td class="num">0.321</td><td class="num">0.269</td><td>+0.052; CI includes 0</td></tr>
<tr><td>Conservative frozen v2 (counts ≥7 days old)</td><td class="num">0.290</td><td class="num">0.231 norm</td><td>95% CI 0.235–0.350; region-year 90% gain CI +0.007 to +0.084</td></tr>
<tr><td>Frozen v2 fed only truly published inputs</td><td class="num">0.291</td><td class="num">—</td><td>Δ +0.001: the 0.290 headline is honest</td></tr>
<tr><td>Operational vs frozen v2</td><td class="num">0.312</td><td class="num">0.290</td><td>+0.022; 90% CI −0.010 to +0.038 (probable, not proven)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Check period 2019–21 (operational)</td><td class="num">0.548</td><td class="num">0.529 norm</td><td>+0.019; CI includes 0</td></tr>
<tr><td>Check pool 2016–21 (conservative v2)</td><td class="num">0.517</td><td class="num">0.499 norm</td><td>a tie (95% CI −0.015 to +0.057)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Brier score (conservative)</td><td class="num">0.0318</td><td class="num">0.0355</td><td>Brier skill +0.11</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<blockquote>“Using only the counts MI had actually published by each Sunday, our outlook ranks the coming fortnight’s Dinophysis ≥100 cells/L weeks clearly better than the seasonal norm: PR-AUC 0.31 vs 0.23 nationally for 2022–26, and 0.34 vs 0.20 in the South-West. It does not yet catch more shellfish closures than MI’s own ≥100 cells/L rule.”</blockquote>

<h2>The baseline gauntlet</h2>
<p>A forecast is only as impressive as the baselines it beats. All on the 2022–26 pool, conservative setting:</p>
<div class="tbl"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Forecast</th><th class="num">PR-AUC</th><th>Comment</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>MI-style threshold rules</td><td class="num">0.08–0.12</td><td>Ranked MI rule 0.182</td></tr>
<tr><td>Week-of-year climatology</td><td class="num">0.124</td><td>Same probability for every station in a given week</td></tr>
<tr><td>“Same as last observed”</td><td class="num">0.144</td><td>Persistence of the last count</td></tr>
<tr><td>Station × week seasonal norm</td><td class="num">0.231</td><td>The main comparator</td></tr>
<tr><td>Active-year empirical-Bayes norm</td><td class="num">0.240</td><td>Norm scaled by this season’s station and regional activity; does not absorb the gain (v2 − EB +0.051)</td></tr>
<tr><td>3-feature logistic</td><td class="num">0.263–0.278</td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>Hierarchical ridge logistic</td><td class="num">0.280</td><td>Within 0.010 of v2; beats v2 within 9 of 11 seasons; also scores 0.312 on published-by-Sunday features</td></tr>
<tr class="hl"><td><strong>Model v2 (LightGBM + Platt)</strong></td><td class="num"><strong>0.290</strong></td><td>Frozen 7 Oct 2026</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<div class="callout"><p>The lesson: <strong>the skill comes from MI’s counts, not from the machine-learning machinery.</strong> A transparent ridge model matches v2 closely, which is why it is the comparator to beat in the 2027 confirmatory season.</p></div>

<h2>By region</h2>
<p>Conservative setting, summed-species target, 2022–2026.</p>
<div class="tbl"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Region</th><th class="num">Positives</th><th class="num">Seasonal norm</th><th class="num">Model</th><th>Gain 95% CI</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr class="hl"><td>SW Kerry / West Cork</td><td class="num">241</td><td class="num">0.203</td><td class="num"><strong>0.294</strong></td><td>+0.051 to +0.138 (demonstrated)</td></tr>
<tr><td>South / South-East</td><td class="num">106</td><td class="num">0.524</td><td class="num">0.457</td><td>−0.132 to −0.009 (worse than norm)</td></tr>
<tr><td>East Irish Sea</td><td class="num">66</td><td class="num">0.462</td><td class="num">0.465</td><td>−0.078 to +0.037</td></tr>
<tr><td>Galway / Connemara</td><td class="num">62</td><td class="num">0.077</td><td class="num">0.090</td><td>−0.013 to +0.042</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mayo</td><td class="num">30</td><td class="num">0.127</td><td class="num">0.191</td><td>−0.031 to +0.156</td></tr>
<tr><td>North-West (Donegal / Sligo)</td><td class="num">27</td><td class="num">0.106</td><td class="num">0.079</td><td>−0.050 to −0.007 (worse than norm)</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Skill is concentrated in the South-West, which also has the most blooms. Elsewhere the model is level with, or worse than, the seasonal norm, and the prototype says so on every forecast.</p>

<h2>By year</h2>
<div class="tbl"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Year</th><th class="num">Positives</th><th class="num">Seasonal norm</th><th class="num">Same as last</th><th class="num">Model (conservative)</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>2016</td><td class="num">219</td><td class="num">0.495</td><td class="num">0.338</td><td class="num">0.554</td></tr>
<tr><td>2017</td><td class="num">200</td><td class="num">0.459</td><td class="num">0.286</td><td class="num">0.474</td></tr>
<tr><td>2018</td><td class="num">276</td><td class="num">0.472</td><td class="num">0.321</td><td class="num">0.503</td></tr>
<tr><td>2019</td><td class="num">388</td><td class="num">0.709</td><td class="num">0.432</td><td class="num">0.668</td></tr>
<tr><td>2020</td><td class="num">243</td><td class="num">0.483</td><td class="num">0.232</td><td class="num">0.441</td></tr>
<tr><td>2021</td><td class="num">147</td><td class="num">0.334</td><td class="num">0.182</td><td class="num">0.374</td></tr>
<tr><td>2022</td><td class="num">130</td><td class="num">0.186</td><td class="num">0.123</td><td class="num"><strong>0.262</strong></td></tr>
<tr><td>2023</td><td class="num">77</td><td class="num">0.153</td><td class="num">0.096</td><td class="num"><strong>0.212</strong></td></tr>
<tr><td>2024</td><td class="num">87</td><td class="num">0.216</td><td class="num">0.085</td><td class="num">0.194</td></tr>
<tr><td>2025</td><td class="num">126</td><td class="num">0.298</td><td class="num">0.125</td><td class="num"><strong>0.365</strong></td></tr>
<tr><td>2026 (to Aug)</td><td class="num">114</td><td class="num">0.492</td><td class="num">0.281</td><td class="num">0.459</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>The conservative model beats the seasonal norm in 7 of 11 years and “same as last observed” in all 11. About half of the pooled gain comes from ranking <em>across</em> years (telling bad years from quiet ones) rather than week-by-week timing within a season: year-averaged, v2 beats the norm by +0.029 (3 of 5 recent years).</p>
<figure><img src="assets/img/model_v2_skill.png" alt="Model v2 skill per year, per region, by target, and reliability"><figcaption>Model v2: PR-AUC by year against baselines (shaded = 2022–26 headline pool), by region, which target is most forecastable, and reliability. The “week-0 known” line is the retired optimistic setting; the honest operational figure is 0.312.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>Robustness: does the gain survive?</h2>
<p>A red team re-ran the headline under many ways of resampling the data. The gain over the norm (+0.060, conservative) stays positive at 90% under every blocking scheme; at 95% the region-year interval just touches zero. In the 2016–21 check pool the gain is small (+0.018) and every interval includes zero.</p>
<figure><img src="assets/img/gain_ci_by_block.png" alt="Gain confidence intervals by bootstrap block type"><figcaption>v2 minus seasonal norm under nine bootstrap schemes. Left: 2022–26 headline pool; right: 2016–21 check pool. Thick bars 90%, thin bars 95%.</figcaption></figure>
<p><strong>Multiple testing:</strong> across 72 logged comparisons, the v2 gain has a Benjamini–Hochberg q of 0.07 using station-level p-values, but 0.60 using the stricter region-year p-value. Both are reported.</p>

<h2>Shellfish closures: the commercial bar</h2>
<p>South-West DSP closures, 2019–2026 (62 closure episodes), with alarm thresholds set honestly on earlier years:</p>
<div class="tbl"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Warning rule</th><th class="num">Closures warned</th><th class="num">False-alarm runs</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>MI rule: ≥100 cells/L at the site last week</td><td class="num">46 / 62</td><td class="num">130</td></tr>
<tr><td>MI rule on published-by-Sunday counts</td><td class="num"><strong>50 / 62</strong></td><td class="num">124</td></tr>
<tr><td>Frozen v2, rolling threshold</td><td class="num">42 / 62</td><td class="num">47</td></tr>
<tr><td>Operational model, rolling threshold</td><td class="num">41 / 62</td><td class="num"><strong>41</strong></td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>An earlier claim that v2 “warned 61 of 62 closures” was withdrawn: random warnings issued at the same alarm volume per area and month catch 58.8 of 62 on average. The model’s real advantage is far fewer false-alarm runs, much of it from staying almost silent in quiet years (0–2 alarms in 2021 and 2023–25, when the MI rule raised 13–48).</p>
<figure><img src="assets/img/closure_null.png" alt="Closure null distribution: random warnings with the same alarm budget"><figcaption>The closure null: how many of the 62 South-West DSP closures random warning sets catch with the same alarm budget as v2 (blue) or the MI rule (orange). Dashed lines = actual.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>Genuine prospective record</h2>
<ul>
 <li>In a pseudo-prospective replay of 2026 (5 Apr–13 Sep), the conservative model was <strong>not</strong> better than the norm: 0.446 vs 0.473.</li>
 <li>The first genuine issue was on 4 Oct 2026 (63 forecasts, hash-logged). First scoring: <strong>25 Oct 2026</strong>.</li>
 <li>2027 is the pre-registered confirmatory season (see <a href="#/roadmap">Roadmap</a>).</li>
</ul>
`});
